1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,412
Open-source maintainers get vulnerability reports they cannot cheaply verify. Mimi reproduces the claim in

2
00:00:05,412 --> 00:00:10,825
isolated Nebius Token Factory Sandboxes, at the exact affected version, and returns a verdict

3
00:00:10,825 --> 00:00:16,237
with receipts. It never auto-closes a report.

4
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:23,109
I load a public example: minimist 1.2.5, CVE 2021 44906. Nemotron Nano extracts the

5
00:00:23,109 --> 00:00:29,177
claim. Tavily looks up public advisories using only the package name and version, never

6
00:00:29,177 --> 00:00:35,245
the report text.

7
00:00:36,055 --> 00:00:41,428
Nemotron Ultra writes up to eight hypotheses, each with a test script and a

8
00:00:41,428 --> 00:00:46,801
pass or fail rule fixed in advance. The environment is built once in a

9
00:00:46,801 --> 00:00:52,174
Token Factory Sandbox, and every hypothesis runs as its own fork, offline, in parallel.

10
00:00:58,174 --> 00:01:02,522
Hypotheses that fire are re-run on the fixed version. This one fires on 1.2.5

11
00:01:02,522 --> 00:01:06,870
and not on 1.2.6, so the tier is Confirmed, with every command and output

12
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:11,218
attached.

13
00:01:12,021 --> 00:01:15,581
Each run shows the exact command, its output, and whether the network was isolated.

14
00:01:15,581 --> 00:01:19,141
The tier is assigned by code from the oracle results, not by a model's

15
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:22,701
opinion.

16
00:01:23,502 --> 00:01:27,980
When a report is fabricated, Mimi says how hard it tried. This invented chalk

17
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:32,459
bug: six hypotheses tested, none triggered. That is a negative result with coverage, not

18
00:01:32,459 --> 00:01:36,937
a closed report. A script that crashes is recorded as inconclusive, never as a

19
00:01:36,937 --> 00:01:41,415
negative.

20
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:46,032
And when an oracle fires on both the affected and the fixed version, Mimi

21
00:01:46,032 --> 00:01:49,845
refuses to confirm and asks a human to look.

22
00:01:50,649 --> 00:01:56,412
Mimi covers npm only, and results vary between runs. In our development runs, three

23
00:01:56,412 --> 00:02:02,174
passes over eleven real vulnerabilities fired an oracle on seven each time, and confirmed

24
00:02:02,174 --> 00:02:07,937
two or three against the fixed version. It is open source under Apache 2.0,

25
00:02:07,937 --> 00:02:13,700
built on Nebius Token Factory, Sandboxes and Nemotron, with Tavily for research.
